# Our scorer: butteraugli crate (~/work/butteraugli) `butteraugli_linear`
#             with ButteraugliParams::default() (intensity_target=80, hf_asymmetry=1.0, xmul=1.0)
# Libjxl scorer: butteraugli_main with `--colorspace RGB_D65_SRG_Rel_Lin --pnorm 3.0`
#                running on PFM files (linear sRGB f32, little-endian)
# Inputs to libjxl: PFMs written from the SAME f32 buffers fed to our crate.

## 4-pair matrix (max_score column from TSV)

##   AA = our crate scoring (ref, ours_recon)    score=3.868879  pnorm3=1.470648
##   AB = libjxl scoring     (ref, ours_recon)    score=3.868882  pnorm3=1.470648
##   BA = our crate scoring (ref, libjxl_recon)  score=4.142091  pnorm3=1.408934
##   BB = libjxl scoring     (ref, libjxl_recon)  score=4.142091  pnorm3=1.408934

## Differences:
##   AA vs AB max_score:    3e-6  (6.5 ppm, sub-f32-ULP marshalling noise via PFM round-trip)
##   BA vs BB max_score:    0     (bit-identical at f64 print precision)
##   pnorm3 AA vs AB:       0     (bit-identical)
##   pnorm3 BA vs BB:       0     (bit-identical)

## Sanity: (ref, ref) → both produce 0.0 exactly.

## VERDICT: BUTTERAUGLI METRIC IS AT PARITY (~0.0001% deviation).
##          SA-B's claim of 2.4× metric divergence is FALSIFIED.
##          The iter-0 buttloop ratio (ours 4.31 vs cjxl 10.24) reported by SA-B at
##          commit 33729573 cannot be the metric. It MUST be that the iter-0
##          reconstructions inside the two encoders' buttloops differ substantially.
##          SA-A (commit 5c99dc20) already showed AC strategies diverge on smooth
##          photos at e9 d=4 (576 partial picks vs cjxl's 0). The strategy-search
##          divergence IS the wedge; SA-B mis-attributed it to the metric.

## Why the final cjxl-recon scores 4.14 here (not 10.24):
##   - SA-B measured iter-0 internal score (first iteration of cjxl's own
##     butteraugli loop on its own intermediate reconstruction)
##   - cjxl's iter-0 reconstruction is a coarse first pass that is much WORSE
##     than its final output (~4.14 here). Across 2-4 buttloop iters cjxl
##     converges to a recon that scores 4.14 on its own metric (and on ours).
##   - The 10.24 number was an INTERNAL iter-0 milestone, not the final
##     measurable cjxl quality on the same source. Comparing iter-0 ours-recon
##     against iter-0 libjxl-recon was a confounded apples-to-oranges.

